WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.105
A parameterized query gives the database
client two distinct inputs:

2
00:00:04.185 --> 00:00:07.790
statement text containing placeholders,
and a collection of values.

3
00:00:07.870 --> 00:00:12.085
The driver applies the database protocol
or binding API so each value is handled as

4
00:00:12.165 --> 00:00:13.998
data in its designated expression.

5
00:00:14.078 --> 00:00:17.377
In a PostgreSQL-style client the
placeholders may be numbered,

6
00:00:17.457 --> 00:00:20.757
while another driver may use question
marks, named parameters,

7
00:00:20.837 --> 00:00:22.548
or a tagged-template interface.

8
00:00:22.628 --> 00:00:24.338
The symbol is not the protection.

9
00:00:24.418 --> 00:00:28.695
The protection is the API contract that
receives structure and values separately.

10
00:00:28.775 --> 00:00:32.319
Use this pattern for ordinary data values
such as strings, integers,

11
00:00:32.399 --> 00:00:35.515
booleans, dates, status values,
foreign-key identifiers,

12
00:00:35.595 --> 00:00:39.505
and pagination limits when the database
supports binding in that position.

13
00:00:39.585 --> 00:00:42.945
Preserve native types instead of
converting every value to text.

14
00:00:43.025 --> 00:00:45.469
Validate values for application
correctness,

15
00:00:45.549 --> 00:00:49.154
then pass them through the driver's
documented parameter mechanism.

16
00:00:49.234 --> 00:00:53.267
Some drivers describe the operation as
prepared execution and others perform

17
00:00:53.347 --> 00:00:57.807
protocol-level parameter binding without a
permanently stored prepared statement;

18
00:00:57.887 --> 00:01:01.308
the relevant security property remains
separation from SQL text.

19
00:01:01.388 --> 00:01:03.221
Manual escaping is a weaker design.

20
00:01:03.301 --> 00:01:07.578
Correct quoting depends on the database
dialect, connection settings, encoding,

21
00:01:07.658 --> 00:01:09.735
data type, and exact syntactic context.

22
00:01:09.815 --> 00:01:13.970
A home-grown replacement for quote
characters cannot cover those combinations

23
00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:16.433
reliably and often corrupts legitimate
data.

24
00:01:16.513 --> 00:01:21.095
Parameterization moves encoding
responsibility to the component that understands the

25
00:01:21.175 --> 00:01:22.214
database protocol.

26
00:01:22.294 --> 00:01:25.165
It should be the default at every
data-access call site,

27
00:01:25.245 --> 00:01:27.628
including code reached only by internal
jobs,

28
00:01:27.708 --> 00:01:31.130
because internal values can also originate
from imports, queues,

29
00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:32.981
or compromised upstream systems.

30
00:01:33.061 --> 00:01:36.788
Do not undermine binding by generating
placeholder text from raw input.

31
00:01:36.868 --> 00:01:41.023
For multiple values, use a documented
array-binding feature or let trusted code

32
00:01:41.103 --> 00:01:45.380
create the required number of placeholder
tokens while appending each value to the

33
00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:46.682
parameter collection.

34
00:01:46.762 --> 00:01:49.939
Never insert the values themselves into
that generated text.

35
00:01:50.019 --> 00:01:54.051
Parameterization also does not make a
query authorized or logically correct;

36
00:01:54.131 --> 00:01:55.964
it guarantees a narrower property.

37
00:01:56.044 --> 00:02:00.260
The supplied values cannot rewrite the SQL
program through that binding channel.
